## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a compact computer vision pipeline with practical on-device adaptation under tight latency constraints. It also probes understanding of data strategy and evaluation beyond single-object pose tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight detection with 2D bounding boxes
- Depth proxy estimation from object size
- Simple tracking with temporal consistency
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-shot)
- Counting metrics and evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: detection + tracking update loop
for (const frame of video) {
  const detections = detector.predict(frame);
  const depthProxy = estimateDepth(detections, calibration);
  const tracked = tracker.update(detections, depthProxy);
  yield { boxes: tracked, count: countPackages(tracked) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces a new angle: multi-sensor fusion with event cameras for high-dynamic scenes. Assesses architectural discipline and concrete trade-offs for low latency and on-device adaptation.

## Key Concepts
- Event cameras and RGB fusion
- 3D voxel grids and lightweight transformers
- On-device 5-shot adaptation and object priors
- Temporal consistency and occlusion handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo code: fuse RGB features with events, then predict pose and mask
function forward(rgbFrame, events, priors){
  const rgbFeat = rgbBackbone(rgbFrame);
  const evtFeat = eventBackbone(events);
  const fused = fuse3D(rgbFeat, evtFeat);
  const pose = poseHead(fused, priors);
  const mask = segHead(fused);
  return {pose, mask};
}
```

